WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:28.230
It was the shot heard around the world. On
June 28, 1914, on a sweltering street in Sarajevo,

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:33.800
a gunman fired into an open-topped car, killing
Archduke Franz Ferdinand. The murder of the

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.660
heir to the Austro-Hungarian Empire was the
push that set the dominoes of Europe falling.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.980
One by one, the great nations declared war,
until countries as far away as India, Japan,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:50.100
and America had all been sucked into the quagmire.
Known as WWI, that quagmire would kill twenty

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.170
million people and change the course of history.
But who was Franz Ferdinand? Who was this

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.860
obscure archduke whose death set the world
ablaze?

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:06.420
Born into a branch of the Austrian royal family,
Franz Ferdinand seemed destined for a life

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:13.470
of rich obscurity. Until, one day, tragic
events conspired to make him heir to his family’s

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.780
empire. As emperor-in-waiting, Ferdinand implemented
reforms and worked on plans that could’ve

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.040
changed everything… only for an assassin’s
bullet to cut him down too soon. In today’s

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.670
Biographics, we’re investigating the life
of the man whose death started WWI… and

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:32.630
considering what might have happened had he
lived.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:39.899
The Two Heirs
At the moment Franz Ferdinad first opened

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:46.700
his eyes, on 18 December, 1863, not even the
greatest seer could’ve predicted the tortured

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.700
route that would lead to him dying on an anonymous
Sarajevo street half a century later.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.560
At the time, Sarajevo was within the orbit
of the decaying Ottoman Empire. Franz Ferdinand,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.939
meanwhile, had been born into the chilly air
of the Ottomans’ ancient enemy: Austria.

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:06.560
Not that being enemies meant the empires were
at war. In fact, they were in a period of

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.940
relative peace that had allowed Ferdinand’s
ostensibly-military family to become lazy

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.340
and boring.
Ferdinand’s father, Archduke Karl Ludwig,

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:21.810
was as staid and conservative as a church
belltower. His mother, Maria Annunziata, was

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.610
a Bourbon princess who’d done nothing of
note.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:30.430
Yet, for all their dull respectability, the
family did have one claim on being interesting.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:35.470
Karl Ludwig was the brother was Franz Josef,
the Habsburg emperor of Austria.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.410
Not that baby Franz Ferdinand got much out
his famous uncle.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.540
Since Franz Josef already had a son - Rudolf
- there was little chance young Ferdinand

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.790
would ever become emperor himself.
Obviously, it wasn’t impossible. He was

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:50.060
third in line.
But it was unlikely enough that Ferdinand

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:54.500
was kept away from the corridors of power,
even as he had to go through the standard

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.237
Habsburg upbringing of God, army, and duty.
In fact, Franz Ferdinand’s upbringing was

00:02:57.237 --> 00:02:58.237
so utterly average that there’s very little
to say about it.

00:02:58.237 --> 00:03:02.300
Perhaps the only way the boy diverged from
the standard Habsburg mould was in his passion

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.531
for botany.
While other Habsburg men valued their guns

00:03:05.531 --> 00:03:10.220
and uniforms, Franz Ferdinand found an unlikely
outlet in flowers.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.220
From early childhood, it’s said that he
never looked quite as content as when he was

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.830
gently pressing a rose between the pages of
a book.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:23.070
Still, it’s not like Ferdinand’s horticultural
side was sign of an artistic spirit.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.730
He really did value guns and uniforms, and
enjoyed his conservative upbringing.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.750
The same cannot be said for Rudolf.
The only son of emperor Franz Josef, Crown

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:38.180
Prince Rudolf had been born on August 21,
1858, ending a run of unwanted baby girls.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:42.670
For Franz Josef, the arrival of a viable heir
had been such a cause for celebration that

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:47.200
he’d made Rudolf commander of an entire
infantry regiment the very next day.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.180
No word on how the previous commander felt
about being replaced by a literally newborn

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.510
baby.
But while his cousin Franz Ferdinand would

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:59.160
enjoy his military upbringing, Rudolf had
hated it from the get-go.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.170
From a young age, Rudolf had been pushed off
onto a a sadistic tutor named Major-General

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:09.120
Count Leopold Gondrecourt.
Gondrecourt was that asshole Phys Ed teacher

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.000
you had as a kid, pumped up on 19th Century
notions of manliness, and given carte blanche

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.739
to do whatever he liked to Rudolf.
The Crown Prince was made to exercise outdoors

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:24.760
for hours in the depths of the Austrian winter.
Guns were fired near his head in the middle

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.550
of the night to wake him up.
By 1865, Rudolf was a mental wreck. But still

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:35.410
Franz Josef refused to fire Gondrecourt, believing
Rudolf was a weakling who needed toughening

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:37.789
up.
Finally, Empress Sisi intervened, threatening

00:04:37.789 --> 00:04:41.660
to leave the royal household if Gondrecourt
kept abusing their son.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:47.039
But by then, the damage had already been done.
Always a sensitive boy, Rudolf would never

00:04:47.039 --> 00:04:50.550
recover from the hard years he spent in the
hands of Count Gondrecourt.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:57.569
It were these mental scars that would soon
entwine his fate with Franz Ferdinand’s.

00:04:57.569 --> 00:05:04.289
Turning Points
By the early 1880s, the two cousins had grown

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:07.949
into very different people.
Off on his family’s estates, Franz Ferdinand

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:13.729
had become exactly the sort of man the newly-reorganized
Austro-Hungarian Empire was designed to churn

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:16.060
out.
He’d joined the army, become a captain within

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.930
a year.
Now he was in his early twenties and climbing

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.050
the ranks, ready to serve his country.
Even his love of flowers had been offset by

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.949
a newfound passion for the masculine sport
of hunting.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:31.960
And no-one hunted quite like Franz Ferdinand.
If it moved, and wasn’t wearing an Austrian

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.960
army uniform then Ferdinand was more than
happy to shoot it. It’s estimated he shot

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.629
over a quarter of a million animals in his
lifetime.

00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:45.330
But, despite the outward success of Ferdinand’s
military upbringing, it hadn’t sanded off

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:49.080
all the rough edges.
Adult Franz Ferdinand was a man with a volcanic

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.830
temper.
Like many rich people, he was used to getting

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:57.099
what he wanted. But whereas most rich people
sulk in private when they don’t, Ferdinand

00:05:57.099 --> 00:06:02.120
sulked openly, for days on end.
It got so bad that his army buddies would

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:05.710
avoid him like most of us would avoid a pedophile
made of ebola.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:09.689
Still, at least Franz Ferdiand was broadly
content.

00:06:09.689 --> 00:06:13.909
The same can’t be said for Rudolf.
In the years since Count Gondrecourt had been

00:06:13.909 --> 00:06:18.960
kicked out on his sadistic backside, Ferdinand’s
cousin had gone from bad to worse.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.099
He’d become melancholic. Alcoholic. Other
“cholics” that medical science hasn’t

00:06:23.099 --> 00:06:27.189
even dreamed of.
Yet even as he emotionally decayed, Rudolf

00:06:27.189 --> 00:06:30.871
intellectually sharpened.
At the time of our story, the Austro-Hungarian

00:06:30.871 --> 00:06:37.190
Empire was home to a kaleidoscope of ethnicities.
There were Czechs. Slovaks. Poles. Italians.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:42.300
Croats. Slovenes. Serbs. Romanians. Ukrainians.
However, only Austrians and Hungarians were

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.370
represented at the top. While that had been
common in empires of the past, it wasn’t

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:51.930
so viable in a Europe now brimming with nationalism.
As future emperor, Rudolf was desperate to

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:55.080
reform things.
Unfortunately, his father’s motto was essentially

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:00.060
“do nothing and hope for the best.”
Franz Josef was slow. Conservative. As suspicious

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.900
of demands for autonomy as he was of his heir’s
reforming zeal.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:08.169
But rather than talk about it, Franz Josef
simply sidelined Rudolf, marrying him off

00:07:08.169 --> 00:07:14.220
to Princess Sophie of Belgium, a woman so
dull she could’ve been used as anesthetic.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.759
Frustrated at court, stuck with a wife he
hated, and still dealing with the scars of

00:07:18.759 --> 00:07:22.569
his abusive childhood, Rudolf began to drift
into a poisonous depression.

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:27.430
Interestingly, it was around this time that
Franz Ferdiand’s life also took a grim turn.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:31.689
In the mid-1880s, the third in line to the
throne was diagnosed with tuberculosis - a

00:07:31.689 --> 00:07:34.662
lung disease that killed many in the 19th
Century.

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:39.279
But things were worse for Rudolf.
While Franz Ferdinand’s disease was something

00:07:39.279 --> 00:07:44.529
that could be understood, Rudolf’s disease
was something that hid away from sight, something

00:07:44.529 --> 00:07:50.430
his family didn’t even try to understand.
It was an attitude that would lead to tragedy.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:56.249
On January 30, 1889, Rudolf - now extremely
depressed - took his 17-year old lover, Baroness

00:07:56.249 --> 00:08:01.259
Mary Vetsera, to his hunting lodge Mayerling.
There, the two signed a suicide pact. Then

00:08:01.259 --> 00:08:04.680
Rudolf shot her through the head before turning
his pistol on himself.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.259
The bullet that passed through Rudolf’s
brain did more than just kill the Crown Prince.

00:08:08.259 --> 00:08:12.509
It ended Franz Josef’s line, extinguishing
his only male heir.

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:18.110
It was a moment when history trembled, then
pivoted on its axis. When the multiverse branched.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:22.800
Poor Franz Ferdinand didn’t know it yet,
but Rudolf’s two gunshots had just set him

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:32.539
on a path to his own, equally-violent destiny.
The Heir is Dead… Long Live the Heir!

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:37.089
Of all the unintended consequences of Rudolf’s
suicide, Franz Ferdinand becoming heir was

00:08:37.089 --> 00:08:41.100
likely the most unintended of all.
That’s because Ferdinand was third in line

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:45.910
to the throne. After Rudolf decided to decorate
Mayerling’s walls with the inside of his

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:49.529
skull, the succession passed to Ferdinand’s
dad, Karl Ludwig.

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:53.160
But Karl wasn’t interested. Within days,
he’d abdicated his claim.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.290
And just like that, Franz Ferdinand - hot-headed,
military, flower-loving Franz Ferdinand - was

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:01.829
the future emperor.
For Emperor Franz Josef, this was a little

00:09:01.829 --> 00:09:06.120
like being forced to swallow a pint of freshly-squeezed
disappointment.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:11.100
Franzes Ferdinand and Josef did not get on.
The emperor was a reserved man who never showed

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:14.820
emotion to anyone but his beloved wife Sisi.
While Franz Ferdinand…

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:21.440
Well, let’s just say the sulking and the
volcanic temper had not improved with age.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.430
Even though Karl Ludwig abdicated his claim,
Franz Josef refused to name Ferdinand his

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:28.570
heir.
He claimed Ferdinand’s tuberculosis made

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:31.769
him unfit to be emperor, that he might die
at any moment.

00:09:31.769 --> 00:09:36.940
So, in 1892, Franz Ferdinand set off for warmer
climes, desperate to relieve his infected

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.170
lungs.
For the next two years, the archduke traveled

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:43.470
the world, seeing the sights, taking the air…
and, of course, shooting everything in sight.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:49.630
Ferdinand shot kangaroos in Australia, tigers
in India, rare birds in Japan.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:54.320
When he returned in 1894, he actually brought
two stuffed elephants he’d shot back with

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.829
him.
Franz Josef’s response? He declared his

00:09:56.829 --> 00:10:01.480
nephew’s hobby was “mass murder.”
But while the trip may have been bad for exotic

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:04.570
wildlife, it had been good for Franz Ferdinand’s
lungs.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:08.500
The heir returned revitalized, better than
he’d been in years. Barely had he reached

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.370
the empire than he was attending balls in
Prague.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:15.330
It was here that the stuffy archduke met Countess
Sophie Chotek.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:19.170
In the entire history of Franz Ferdinand’s
life, there was almost nobody who remembered

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:24.890
him fondly. He was too quick to anger, too
prone to sulking, too much of an asshole.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:29.460
The one exception was Sophie Chotek.
Somehow, this Bohemian countess brought out

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:36.180
a side in this gruff, unlikeable man that
no-one else ever saw. A side that was tender.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.839
Respectful.
Maybe Sophie was the closest human equivalent

00:10:38.839 --> 00:10:43.990
Ferdinand ever found to one of his flowers.
Something rare and radiant that made him feel

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:49.449
things he otherwise never experienced.
Naturally, Franz Josef hated her.

00:10:49.449 --> 00:10:54.510
Sophie may have been rich, but she was a mere
countess. For the snobbish Habsburgs, that

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:57.621
just wasn’t posh enough.
So the emperor forbid their union. Pushed

00:10:57.621 --> 00:11:01.259
his new heir away. Just as he’d once pushed
Rudolf away.

00:11:01.259 --> 00:11:04.630
Unlike Rudolf, though, Franz Ferdinand was
willing to fight.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:08.290
All through that miserable decade, the heir
argued with the emperor.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:13.420
He argued with him as Karl Ludwig succumbed
to typhoid fever. As empress Sisi was felled

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:18.480
by an assassin in Geneva.
Finally, in 1900, Ferdinand hit jackpot.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.990
After years of cajoling, Pope Leo XIII, Kaiser
Wilhelm II, and Tsar Nicholas II all pleaded

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:28.709
to Franz Josef on Ferdiand’s behalf.
Faced with the head of his religion, the head

00:11:28.709 --> 00:11:32.660
of his closest allied nation, and the head
of the nation he desperately wanted to be

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:36.820
allied with all appealing to him, Franz Josef
at last relented.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:42.370
The emperor allowed Ferdinand and Sophie Chotek
to marry that very year. But there was a catch.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:47.019
Before he could tie the knot, Franz Ferdinand
was made to sign an oath, excluding both his

00:11:47.019 --> 00:11:50.920
children from the line of succession, and
Sophie from all official ceremonies.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:55.440
It was a high price to pay. But it was also
the only offer Franz Ferdinand would ever

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:00.080
get. Reluctantly, he agreed.
It’s doubtful anyone realized it, but at

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:05.540
that moment, the multiverse branched yet again,
taking the empire ever-further from peace,

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.759
from survival.
Franz Josef may not have realized it, but

00:12:08.759 --> 00:13:11.209
his obstinacy had just doomed them all.
The Spirit of Rudolf

00:13:11.209 --> 00:13:59.589
“Those who

00:13:59.589 --> 00:14:03.779
fail to learn from history are doomed to repeat
it”, is one of those misquotes that has

00:14:03.779 --> 00:14:08.670
been attributed to just about everybody.
But, in the context of early 20th Century

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:14.449
Austria-Hungary, it is absolutely something
Franz Josef should’ve kept in mind.

00:14:14.449 --> 00:14:18.180
Having contributed to Rudolf’s breakdown
by isolating him at court and forcing him

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:22.199
to marry someone he had nothing in common
with, the emperor was now turning his guns

00:14:22.199 --> 00:14:24.720
on his heir.
The marriage of Franz Ferdinand and Sophie

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:29.459
Chotek had been a personal insult to Franz
Josef. He instructed everyone at court to

00:14:29.459 --> 00:14:33.600
ignore Ferdinand’s new wife.
At the same time, he began pushing his nephew

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:37.300
away from the business of government, castrating
him politically.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:41.649
Rudolf had tried to reform the empire, but
been sidelined. And now Franz Ferdinand was

00:14:41.649 --> 00:14:47.300
about to suffer the same fate.
Why did Franz Josef so singularly fail both

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:51.960
his heirs? There’s a lot of debate about
this, but one possible reason is that he ascended

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:56.870
to the throne when he was just 18, and simply
wasn’t aware that most future emperors needed

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:01.050
guidance, needed teaching.
When Ferdinand tried to follow in Rudolf’s

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:05.439
footsteps and modernize the empire, he got
neither of those things. The emperor just

00:15:05.439 --> 00:15:07.879
blanked him.
And this was a problem, because - at the dawn

00:15:07.879 --> 00:15:13.211
of the 20th Century - Franz Ferdiand was the
only one with ideas for holding the Austro-Hugarian

00:15:13.211 --> 00:15:16.240
Empire together.
Like Rudolf, Ferdinand had become obsessed

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.220
with the the issue of nationalism in the multi-ethnic
empire.

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:23.630
But while Rudolf had tried to implement minor
reforms, Ferdinand wanted to overhaul the

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:27.079
whole system.
Franz Ferdinand’s plans ranged from splitting

00:15:27.079 --> 00:15:32.870
the empire into a United States of Austria,
to elevating the South Slavs (AKA the Slovenes,

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:37.240
Croats, and Serbs) to joint-ruler status alongside
the Austrians and Hungarians.

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:42.689
But don’t go thinking Ferdinand was a Rudolf-style
liberal, oh no.

00:15:42.689 --> 00:15:47.769
The man was a reactionary conservative who
hated Hungarians, hated Slavs, wasn’t all

00:15:47.769 --> 00:15:53.709
that hot on Jews, and thought Austria had
a God given right to stomp all over its minorites.

00:15:53.709 --> 00:15:59.490
He was, in short, a very unpleasant dude.
But here’s the thing about unpleasant dudes.

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:03.199
They can sometimes do the right thing for
the wrong reason.

00:16:03.199 --> 00:16:07.230
Franz Ferdinad’s plans for liberalizing
the empire, granting all men the vote, and

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:11.709
giving each nationality more autonomy may
have come from a cynical calculation that

00:16:11.709 --> 00:16:15.930
it was the only way to keep his family in
power, but it really would’ve improved things

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.970
for millions.
It’s not beyond the realms of possibility

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:23.480
that a reformed, federal Austria-Hungary would’ve
survived right up to today.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:29.790
But we’ll never know for sure, because Franz
Josef nixed every single plan Ferdinand had.

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:34.250
In the court, these stifled reforms turned
Franz Ferdinand into a laughing stock.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:39.220
It was whispered in Vienna that the heir was
clinically insane, that his family’s inbreeding

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:43.279
had caught up with him.
For a while, Ferdiand tried to brave the storm.

00:16:43.279 --> 00:16:48.100
But even this insanely unpopular man wasn’t
immune to public opinion.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:51.879
In the mid-1900s, Ferdinand and Sophie abruptly
quit Vienna.

00:16:51.879 --> 00:16:56.509
They retired to their home of Konopiště
in what is now the Czech Republic, and settled

00:16:56.509 --> 00:17:01.189
down to a life of domestic bliss.
It was almost a happy ending. Together, the

00:17:01.189 --> 00:17:05.940
couple had three children; and Franz Ferdinand
settled back into his old passion of horticulture,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.360
creating one of the world’s largest rose
gardens.

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:13.180
In another time, in another place, that might
have been the end of things: with Sophie a

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:18.791
happy mother, and Ferdinand quietly pressing
his roses by candlelight, all the cares of

00:17:18.791 --> 00:17:22.850
empire forgotten.
But this was Europe in the early 20th Century,

00:17:22.850 --> 00:17:29.059
a tinderbox less than a decade away from exploding.
For Franz Ferdinand and Sophie there would

00:17:29.059 --> 00:17:36.590
be no peace. The wheels were already in motion.
Their violent fate was waiting for them.

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:44.570
Death in Sarajevo
On 28 June, 1914, Archduke Franz Ferdinand

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:49.080
and his wife Sophie disembarked from a train
in the Bosnian city of Sarajevo and stepped

00:17:49.080 --> 00:17:52.590
into an open-topped car.
It was a hot day and the river running through

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:56.850
the center of the city was lower than usual.
But as the motorcade whipped past, it wasn’t

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:58.590
the river the archduke should’ve been looking
at.

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:02.100
It was the crowds.
Near the city’s old Latin Bridge, Nedeljko

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:07.659
Cabrinovic and Gavrilo Princip waited, anonymous
faces amid the assembled masses.

00:18:07.659 --> 00:18:12.460
In their pockets of their heavy coats sat
bombs and guns. Near their trembling fingertips

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:16.520
lay cyanide capsules.
They and their comrades had one goal: to kill

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:21.500
the heir of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.
To understand why, we need to quickly wind

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:25.820
back Father Time’s videotape.
Remember when we told you Sarajevo was part

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:29.530
of the decaying Ottoman Empire?
Well, fifteen years after Franz Ferdinand’s

00:18:29.530 --> 00:18:34.600
birth, in 1878, the Ottoman Empire had completely
decayed in the Balkans, losing nearly all

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.690
its territories.
Into this power vacuum the Austro-Hungarians

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:41.760
had marched, taking over Bosnia on the strict
understanding that they were only there to

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:45.779
keep the region from imploding.
But Vienna and Budapest had no intention of

00:18:45.779 --> 00:18:50.299
ever leaving. In 1908, they’d officially
annexed the region. But rather than passing

00:18:50.299 --> 00:18:53.510
virtually unnoticed, it had caused a European
crisis.

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:57.919
An outraged Russia had permanently turned
its back on Austria-Hungary. In the Balkans,

00:18:57.919 --> 00:19:01.340
Serbs had declared the empire their mortal
enemy.

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:04.580
And now Cabrinovic and Princip were determined
to make the empire pay.

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:07.870
Interestingly, they nearly didn’t get their
chance.

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:12.020
The night before, Franz Ferdinand had had
a premonition that all would not be well in

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:14.970
Sarajevo.
But it was his and Sophie’s wedding anniversary,

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:20.029
and these semi-unofficial trips with minimal
security were the only ones Franz Josef allowed

00:19:20.029 --> 00:19:23.700
Sophie to go on.
So here the couple were, speeding alongside

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:28.900
the half-dry river in Sarajevo, blissfully
unaware of the men waiting to kill them.

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:33.340
As the car roared past the Latin Bridge, Cabrinovic
whipped out a bomb and hurled it.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:38.130
But the bomb bounced off the car and hit the
police escort behind, blowing them up instead.

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:42.850
In panic, Cabrinovic forgot his gun and tried
to commit suicide by leaping into the river…

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:48.130
...only to land on the dried-out bank, missing
the water and breaking his legs.

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:53.520
As Gavrilo Princip watched in a daze, the
car carrying his and Cabrinovic’s target

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:59.110
motored away at speed. He’d failed.
Except, as you already know, he hadn’t.

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:02.350
What came next was like something out of a
tragic farce.

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:06.419
The car carrying Ferdiand and Sophie stopped
at the City Hall, as scheduled. But rather

00:20:06.419 --> 00:20:12.460
than focus on getting the hell away from Sarajevo,
everyone carried on like nothing had happened.

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:16.570
Once the ceremony was over, Franz Ferdinand
even insisted on driving back into town to

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:19.740
check on the wounded.
As someone who’d been there that day later

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:22.309
said:
“We all felt awkward, because we knew that

00:20:22.309 --> 00:20:26.870
when he went out he would certainly be killed.”
Yet no-one stopped the archduke from getting

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:31.450
back in the car. No-one told the driver to
take a different route in case more assassins

00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:34.500
were waiting.
And no-one raised a finger as Ferdinand and

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:39.880
Sophie headed back towards the Latin Bridge,
where Gavrilo Princip was still standing in

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:44.260
shock, unable to believe he’d missed his
chance to assassinate the Austrian heir.

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:49.130
One can only wonder how he felt when he saw
the car motoring back toward him, like a moving

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:55.070
target going round a shooting-range carousel,
giving him another shot at the grand prize.

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:59.690
It was a shot Princip gladly took.
The first bullet hit Sophie, killing her almost

00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:04.860
instantly. The second thudded into Franz Ferdinand’s
chest, turning his bright blue uniform a brilliant

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:07.500
red.
As the crowd set upon Princip, the car sped

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:10.700
away from the scene.
In the back, Franz Ferdinand held poor, dead

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:13.210
Sophie Chotek, whispering in her lifeless
ear.

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:17.529
“Sophie, Sophie, don’t die! Stay alive
for the children.”

00:21:17.529 --> 00:21:21.210
As the blood seeped through Ferdinand’s
tunic, a bodyguard asked if he was hurt.

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:26.919
“It’s nothing,” the heir said, irritable
to the last. “It’s nothing… it’s nothing…”

00:21:26.919 --> 00:21:29.760
Those were the last words Franz Ferdinand
ever uttered.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:34.200
As news broke that evening that the heir to
the Austrian throne had been assassinated,

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:38.640
Europe plunged into a state of shock. In every
capital, in every embassy, people wondered

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:43.700
what it might mean. If it even meant anything.
But make no mistake.

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:47.940
This was old Europe’s 9/11, the moment that
would change everything.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:53.539
In just over one month’s time, the entire
continent would be at war.

00:21:53.539 --> 00:21:59.910
The Long March to War
Although the life of Franz Ferdinand ended

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:04.330
that hot June day, his story didn’t.
In the aftermath of the assassination, Austrian

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:08.500
intelligence reported the attack had been
organized by the Serbian government. Vienna

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:12.630
nearly invaded that same week.
Today, some historians think this could’ve

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:17.060
prevented WWI. Sympathy initially lay with
Austria, and a quick strike might’ve been

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:20.640
seen as justified.
But the realities of the empire meant Budapest

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:25.840
had to be consulted first. And both capitals
wound up dithering until the shock had faded,

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:29.830
by which point Russia was reasserting its
military alliance with Serbia.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:32.850
In Vienna, Emperor Franz Josef agonized over
what to do.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:37.610
He’d now lost his second heir in a violent,
unforeseen incident. But unlike Rudolf’s

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:42.169
suicide, this time there was a physical enemy,
someone the Austrians could get revenge on.

00:22:42.169 --> 00:22:47.060
By July, the drumbeat of war echoing over
Europe was unmistakable. On the 28th, Franz

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:51.860
Josef officially signed a document declaring
war on Serbia. Austrian troops marched that

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:55.410
same day.
Yet even now, WWI was not inevitable.

00:22:55.410 --> 00:22:59.440
It wasn’t until Russia began to mobilize
to defend Serbia that Europe hit the point

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:02.500
of no return.
The sight of Russia mobilizing scared the

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:05.590
Germans, who in turn began mobilizing against
the Russians.

00:23:05.590 --> 00:23:09.380
The French then got double scared and began
to mobilize against the Germans, which led

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:14.159
to the German decision to attack France first
via Belgium, which in turn dragged Britain

00:23:14.159 --> 00:23:17.559
into the war.
It was the dominoes falling, one by one, adding

00:23:17.559 --> 00:23:20.419
up to a chain reaction that couldn’t be
stopped.

00:23:20.419 --> 00:23:25.679
By August, Europe was at war. It wouldn’t
end until 20 million were dead, and Austro-Hungary,

00:23:25.679 --> 00:23:29.309
the Ottoman Empire, and Tsarist Russia had
all collapsed into dust.

00:23:29.309 --> 00:23:35.210
Not that Franz Josef lived to see it.
The elderly empire died in 1916, halfway through

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:37.760
the war.
It’s possible that, on his deathbed, he

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:42.760
looked back on both Rudolf and Franz Ferdinand,
his two dead heirs, and wondered if their

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:46.460
reforms might’ve saved his empire.
The last time Franz Ferdinand appeared in

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:50.640
history as anything other than the trigger
for WWI, it was 1917.

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:55.179
That year, a huge statue of him was unveiled
in Sarajevo, on the very spot he was killed.

00:23:55.179 --> 00:24:00.320
It was a heartfelt tribute from the city’s
Austrians. It was also a short-lived one.

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:05.220
After WWI ended and Austria collapsed, the
statue was destroyed. Later, under Tito’s

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:10.940
Yugoslavia, it was replaced by monuments celebrating
not the murdered Franz Ferdinand, but his

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:15.020
assassin, Gavrilo Princip.
Today, Franz Ferdinand has been almost entirely

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:18.210
forgotten.
Oh, sure, his name is famous, his place in

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:22.800
the history books assured.
But the Ferdinand we remember is not a full

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:28.370
person, but a symbol. A single image of a
portly man in silly clothes sat in an open-topped

00:24:28.370 --> 00:24:32.550
car, seconds before his death shattered an
entire world.

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:36.159
His face and name may remain, but his soul
has been forgotten.

00:24:36.159 --> 00:24:41.150
It’s said that some men are born to greatness,
while others have greatness thrust upon them.

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:46.740
The life of Franz Ferdinand belongs to another,
much-less known category. One of men who were

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:52.309
not great, or even particularly notable, but
without whom we wouldn’t have the modern

00:24:52.309 --> 00:24:54.659
world.
He may have been moody, he may have been hard

00:24:54.659 --> 00:24:58.220
to love. But Franz Ferdinand was also a human
being.

00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:02.309
Next time November rolls around and you see
someone wearing a poppy, spare a thought for

00:25:02.309 --> 00:25:23.809
the accidental heir. For the man who, in death,
unwittingly changed history.

